        <section id="section-windows-vsi-server">
          <h2 id="windows-vsi-server">Windows virtual server instance requirements</h2>
          <p>If you have a firewall, you must allow your Windows virtual server instance to access to WSUS server IP address ranges as follows; otherwise, updates and licensing do not function properly.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>wsustok0401.service.softlayer.com is 10.201.177.200</li>
            <li>wsustok0201.service.softlayer.com is 10.3.65.50</li>
            <li>wsustok0501.service.softlayer.com is 10.201.193.200</li>
          </ul>
          <table>
            <caption caption-side="bottom">Windows virtual server instance server requirements</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Data Center</th>
                <th>City</th>
                <th>BCR IP Range</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>tok04</td>
                <td>Tokyo</td>
                <td>10.3.17.0/24<br> 10.192.0.0/16
                  <br> 10.201.187.0/24
                </td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p>For more information about locating your WSUS server in the Windows registry by using a registration key, see <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/deployment/update/waas-wu-settings#configuring-automatic-updates-by-editing-the-registry"
              target="_blank" class="external">Configuring Automatic Updates by editing the registry</a> or <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/de-de/security-updates/windowsupdateservices/21669493" target="_blank" class="external">Configure Clients in a Non–Active Directory Environment</a>.</p>
          <hr class="footnotes-sep">
          <section class="footnotes">
            <ol class="footnotes-list">
              <li id="fn1" class="footnote-item">
                <p>The 10.1.129.0/24 subnet, within the 10.1.128.0/19 master subnet, is used for Global service virtual IPs, which are not located in dal05. <a href="#fnref1" class="footnote-backref">↩︎</a></p>
              </li>
              <li id="fn2" class="footnote-item">
                <p class="note">Directionality is from the customer compute perspective. Outbound means leaving your account towards the service. Inbound means service reaching out to compute. <a href="#fnref2" class="footnote-backref">↩︎</a></p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </section>
        </section>
      </section>
